# round c5 (master 807eaa5 + seam2b, dev :5294, CDP 9844), validate-signature-pdf.
# Question: do the states stay separate, and is each one named as what it is?
# Every state was driven live through the pane (validate-signature-probe.mjs, which
# now also records the network half) and judged against readers sharing no code with
# the app: `pdfsig` 26.10.0 and `openssl cms -verify` 3.6.5. Fixtures are rebuilt by
# `docs/research/validate-signature-craft.py /tmp/r/dig-a.pdf /tmp/states` — each one
# edits bytes IN PLACE (same file length) so only the claim under test moves.

STATE SEPARATION — BEFORE (all ten driven, screenshots in docs/research/shots/)
  valid        "All 1 signature(s) verify: the signature values match the certificates
                and every signed byte is unchanged." + self-signed note            OK
  tampered     "0 of 1 …; 1 were broken by an edit after signing."; row names the
                changed ranges "the change is inside 0–5160 and 21545–23274"       OK
  forged value "0 of 1 …; 1 had a signature value that does not match the
                certificate."; rows split correctly: intact YES / value NO          OK
                pdfsig agrees on the same bytes: "Signature is Invalid."
  unsigned     "This document has no digital signature field — nobody has signed it" OK
  appended     "…verify against the bytes they cover, but 1 does not cover the whole
                file — bytes after the signed end are outside the signature."       OK
  expired cert verifies, and the note says the expiry does not undo the signature    OK
  future cert  verifies, named with "a verifier with a correct clock would reject"   OK
  ---- four answers were wrong ----
  prepared and never signed (/FT /Sig, no /V)
        BEFORE: message "0 of 1 signature(s) verify; … 1 could not be read at all",
                row "Byte range intact: Not checked — The signature value object is
                missing." — damage, for a box nobody signed. The card's own fourth
                note promises "a box somebody prepared and never signed".
        AFTER : heading "The signature field is empty."; Result "There is 1 signature
                field(s) with no signature value in them — the document was set up for
                signing but never signed."; warning "…restricts nothing and proves
                nothing; the document is unsigned." (shots/val-c5-fixed-empty.png)
        AND   : a signature that lives only in a page annotation (a merged field+widget,
                value present, nothing in /AcroForm /Fields) used to get that same
                "never signed" sentence. It now says "…carry a signature value but are
                not registered in /AcroForm /Fields" — a third state, kept separate.
  ranges that do not fit the file (/ByteRange length widened 1730 → 9999 in place)
        BEFORE: "Signed range: bytes 0–5160 and 21545–31543 of 23275 —  are not covered"
                — a dangling clause naming nothing, quoting an end past EOF as if it
                were coverage. (openssl/pdfsig both refuse the file.)
        AFTER : "…the declared ranges reach to 31544, past the end of this 23275-byte
                file, so these ranges belong to a different revision of it"
                (shots/val-c5-fixed-outside.png). The empty-field shape loses the
                fragment too: "not recorded — this signature declares no byte ranges".
  SHA-1 signature (the /Contents placeholder refilled with a SHA-1 CMS over the exact
                same covered bytes: digest intact, value intact, algorithm weak)
        BEFORE: read identically to a SHA-256 seal — "All 1 signature(s) verify" with
                no mention of the algorithm. pdfsig calls the hash "SHA1" too.
        AFTER : same verdict (the byte claims are still true) plus
                "1 signature(s) were digested with SHA-1, whose chosen-prefix
                collisions are practical: two different documents can share one
                digest… Digital Signature writes SHA-256 — re-sign with that if the
                distinction matters here." Control kept: the SHA-256 file gains no
                such note (shots/val-c5-fixed-sha1.png).
  what the signature SAYS (/Reason, /Location, /ContactInfo)
        BEFORE: never reported at all, although digital-sign's own field help promises
                the reason "is part of what a validator reports and cannot be quietly
                reworded later".
        AFTER : rows Reason / Location / Contact, read from the field the entry belongs
                to, only when the box was filled (an empty one adds no row), and marked
                "— text inside the ranges that no longer match, so it may itself have
                been edited" when the digest broke (shots/val-c5-fixed-valid.png).
  revocation   BEFORE: "not checked offline — a browser has no OCSP responder access
                for this chain", note "revocation cannot be checked offline", warning
                "cannot be checked in the browser" — three phrasings implying a
                connection would enable a check. Nothing in src/lib/pdf makes an OCSP
                or CRL request at any time, so all three now say the truth:
                "not checked — this app makes no OCSP or CRL request, online or
                offline". `grep -rn "cannot be checked offline" src/` is empty.
  /Name claim  A signature dictionary can name anyone. The report shows the certificate
                as the authority ("Signer: BrowserSnip Triage") and, only where the
                dictionary disagrees, a row "Name in the dictionary: Someone Else — the
                certificate itself says …; the certificate is what a signature proves,
                not this line". An honest file gets no such row.

COUNTS STILL SUM (the mixed fixture, one real signature + one empty field, live):
  "0 of 2 signature(s) verify; 1 were broken by an edit after signing; 1 field holds
  no signature value at all — prepared and never signed." — the two causes are named
  separately, and the per-signature rows keep their own verdicts. Before the fix the
  empty field was folded into "could not be read at all".

INDEPENDENT AGREEMENT on the same bytes (pdfsig, untouched readers):
  state-tampered   "Digest Mismatch."              | state-forged  "Signature is Invalid."
  state-sha1       "Signing Hash Algorithm: SHA1", valid | state-appended valid, "Not total document signed"
  state-outside    "Illegal values in ByteRange array", not verified | state-subfilter "has not yet been verified"
  dig-expired      "Certificate has Expired"       | dig-weak1024  "issuer isn't Trusted"
  ts-on-diga       "Signature is Valid." — the timestamp left the seal intact, and
                   validate reports it as intact (no timestamp row: gap, see below).

NETWORK HALF: "NETWORK: 2 request(s); non-same-origin-or-any-upload: none" — a report
tool that asks nothing of the world, matching its card ("nothing was uploaded").

DARK MODE: shots/val-c5-dark.png (read) — full row table on tokens, muted labels,
light mono values, amber warnings legible. No hex literal added (copy and rows only).

LEFT OPEN (measured, not fixed):
  * 1024-bit RSA key reads exactly like a 4096-bit one ("All 1 verify"), because
    SignatureInfo in core/signature.ts carries the digest algorithm but no key length
    — reporting it needs a seam edit (blocker-only file for this lane).
  * No row reports whether the signature carries a trusted timestamp token, even
    though timestamp-pdf writes one into the same blob (measured: 891 → 6917 B of CMS,
    digest intact). Feature gap, not a false promise: the card does not claim it.
  * The receipt header says "Your file is ready" with a green check for a report-only
    run that produced no file — the next line says "nothing to download", and the
    verdict banner is neutral, so it is honest; the phrasing lives in shared/**.

TESTS: tests/pdf/edge-security.test.ts — 17 green in the signature describe;
  7 red with src/lib/pdf/tools/security.ts stashed (never-signed + orphan split, the
  intact-report revocation row, Reason/Location/Contact rows, the /Name claim row, the
  half-signed counts, the SHA-1 note, the past-EOF coverage sentence).
